A||||4||Voytek||Delco CD player install - please help!||||1||
Z||000000||Voytek||05-04-2001||04:00 PM||||I bought a DELCO CD player (I think it's a chevy one - not that it makes a difference) and I'm trying to get it all hooked up in my Fiero.  Everything works fine (I got the adapter plugs, etc.) EXCEPT: The built-in sub does not work  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif">  Well, it does make an ugly continuous noise but that's about it.  Has anyone ever figured that out before?  I saw an older post where TGowens says there's a trick to make this work.  Anyone know what that trick is?  I NEED MY BASS - PLEASE HELP!!!!!<BR>Thanks to anyone with info (or suggestions).||||reg||1
Z||000001||DRH||05-04-2001||04:36 PM||||The problem is probably the 2 pink wires going to the sub amp. The amp uses one of these wires to actually power the sub and the other as an on/off switch. The stock radio provides enough power to power the sub. Most aftermarket and probably your delco radio only put out enough ampreage to power a relay. <P>A quick fix is to run a hot when key is On or Accy wire to the 2 pink wires.<P>OR - figure out which is which and wire the power one to hot with key on/accy and the other one where it is. (Trial and error will work, if it's backwards it will do what it's doing now.)<P>OR - wire in a relay that powers both pink wires when the radio is on. <P>||||reg||1
Z||000002||mrfixit58||05-04-2001||05:04 PM||||Ya know... I've been through this and for the life of me, I can't remember how I got it to work. I do know that someone on this forum told he how. It may have been Orief or Ogre. You may try a search on "sub-woofer" or even "factory sub-woofer" and see if one of the tobics pop up. I did mine about 10 months ago.<P>Not too much help,<BR>Roy  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||4
Z||000003||Voytek||05-04-2001||06:49 PM||||I appreciate the help guys.  I will give this a try (one more 'search' and the 'hot wire' thingy.  Thanks.||||reg||7
Z||000004||wkayl||05-04-2001||08:24 PM||||When installing an aftermarket radio, use a harness that adapts to the harness already in the car so you won't have to cut off the factory plug. This installation below applies to people who use the factory harness adapter. Relays are normally numbered 30, 85, 86, 87, 87a. They are marked on the relay. <BR>On number 30.......wire to Battery +, or constant<BR>On number 85.......wire to Ground<BR>On number 86.......wire to Power antenna input on the radio<BR>On number 87.......wire to Power antenna input on the harness adapter<BR>			you will notice that on the other side of the <BR>			adapter on the factory harness, that there is<BR>			two pink wires that go into the plug, these are<BR>			the wires that turn on the subwoofer.<BR>On number 87a......no wire <P>For people who cut off the factory plug, just splice the two pink wires into a wire and plug it into number 87 on the relay. All other wires are the same. <P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/twocars.jpg"> ||||reg||1
